==Old English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|ang|gem-pro|*tanþs}} (by loss of the ''n'' and the resulting [[w:compensatory lengthening|compensatory lengthening]] of the vowel), from {{der|ang|ine-pro|*h₃dónts||tooth}}. Cognates with Old Frisian {{m|ofs|tōth}}, Old Saxon {{m|osx|tand}}, Dutch {{m|nl|tand}}, Old High German {{m|goh|zan}}, {{m|goh|zand}} (German {{m|de|Zahn}}), Old Norse {{m|non|tǫnn}} (Swedish {{m|sv|tand}}), Gothic {{m|got|𐍄𐌿𐌽𐌸𐌿𐍃}}; and with Sanskrit {{m|sa|दन्त}} (Hindi {{m|hi|दांत़}}), Ancient Greek {{m|grc|ὀδών}}, Latin {{m|la|dens}} (French {{m|fr|dent}}), Old Irish {{m|sga|dét}}, Lithuanian {{m|lt|dantìs}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/toːθ/|lang=ang}}

